      "kind": "check",
      "check": {
        "qid": "owf-1",
        "question": "Why must the adversary be quantified before n in the definition of one-wayness?",
        "options": [
          {
            "text": "Because adversaries are faster on short inputs.",
            "correct": false
          },
          {
            "text": "Because a fixed adversary must fail for all sufficiently large input lengths.",
            "correct": true
          },
          {
            "text": "Because n is chosen by the adversary.",
            "correct": false
          },
          {
            "text": "Because polynomial time is only defined asymptotically.",
            "correct": false
          }
        ],
        "explanation": "Since the probability is over x \\leftarrow \\{0,1\\}^n, the guarantee has to hold\nas n grows — an adversary that inverts only finitely many lengths still counts\nas a failure. Fixing the adversary first is what makes \"for all sufficiently\nlarge n\" meaningful."
      }
    },
